+    - Cheaper: the layout (`assemble`) runs ONCE on flat ground (`BASE` 64) and the search only moves it (was one assembly per place, 169 of them, each reading the ground); `assemble` lost its `off` parameter. The search reads coarse terrain (`Ground::cell`: per 4 x 4 block cell of `generate_far` the lowest, the highest and whether any column is wet, 96 bytes a chunk, was 512) and `judge` checks cells, not points (was 3 reads at every 2 blocks of the rigid boxes plus 44, per box): the pad cells first, which fail most and need the fewest chunks; places are 41 (rings of 16 / 32 / 48 blocks, integer cos / sin table) instead of 169. `flatten` no longer asks `generate_far` for the natural height of its columns (it generated up to 4 chunks again for every cell near a start): the column's own ground is read from the `Region` (`natural_ground`, trees and plants skipped), which is free and is the real surface (caves included). The scan of a margin column stops at the first block that is not loose instead of running to the top of the 384-block world.
